F.18.5.

This is a wrapper around unnest, which does the same thing for any array type. F.19.2. Sample Uses
Many database systems have the notion of a one to many table. Such a table usually sits between two
indexed tables, for example:
CREATE TABLE left (id INT PRIMARY KEY, ...);
CREATE TABLE right (id INT PRIMARY KEY, ...);
CREATE TABLE one_to_many(left INT REFERENCES left, right INT
 REFERENCES right);
It is typically used like this:
SELECT right.* from right JOIN one_to_many ON (right.id =
 one_to_many.right)
  WHERE one_to_many.left = item;
This will return all the items in the right hand table for an entry in the left hand table. This is a very
common construct in SQL.

